# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, and auditing our own pre-registration

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Re-testing a robot-learning paper does not need a lab. For ${{cost}} of compute on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX, we re-tested the central claims of recent papers under pass marks committed before each run, and every published number rebuilds from stored rows. Of the {{c_paper}} paper claims tested, {{c_paper_repro}} held on this hardware. The failures fit three proposed classes, each named after the condition the claim did not survive. The clearest is the clock: methods that work on a desktop GPU but miss the real-time budget on the edge board. The other two are zero-shot judges beaten by a trivial signal and latent planners beaten by the commanded joints. A blind second reader still has to confirm the classes. We also audited our own pre-registration by commit ancestry, and the audit shows where our ordering holds and where the repository cannot prove it.

## 1. The register

A robot-learning paper reports what happened on one setup. Of the {{papers}} papers here, {{rig_sim}} were shown in simulation and {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k, while {{rig_5to20k}} used a $5–20k setup, {{rig_lt5k}} one below $5k, and {{rig_unstated}} does not say. Ours costs €{{rig_eur}}: an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB ({{rig_unpriced}} small parts are not yet priced).

The register grew out of our own write-ups, {{l0}} so far, each published with its data. This paper collects them in one table and audits how the table was kept.

Take one entry. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the task's phases, long in transport and short at the grasp. On our edge GPU a single decision takes {{ex_a_ours}}. The budget is {{ex_a_bar}}. So the entry records a fail on this rig, which says nothing against the method on the hardware it was shown on, and beside the verdict it keeps the claim in the paper's words, our pass mark, our measurement and the published rows that rebuild it.

Before a learned model gets credit, the cheapest signal it must beat runs on the same rows: copy-last for a predictor, the commanded joints for a latent, episode length for a judge. A method that loses to that signal has not shown what it claims, however good its own number looks. The threshold itself is committed before the run, and §2 checks how often that held.

We chose the papers for the stack this rig runs (world models, VLA policies, failure monitors, chunking, edge latency) and entered each one before testing it. The next ones are already listed in the register file.

## 2. Auditing our own pre-registration

To rule out moving the goalposts after the fact, a gate's threshold had to be in version control before its result. Notes and commit dates can be edited by hand. Commit ancestry cannot, short of rewriting history that others have already cloned, so the order of each gate is read from `git merge-base --is-ancestor` alone, between the first commit that contains its bar and the first that contains its result. Of the {{pr_rows}} gates logged by 2026-10-31, **{{pr_bar_first}} are bar-first** and {{pr_result_first}} result-first. The rest split into {{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar and {{pr_not_run}} with a threshold but no result yet. Same-commit says nothing about which was written first. The log has since grown to {{pr_today}} rows ({{pt_bar_first}} bar-first, {{pt_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pt_result_first}} result-first, {{pt_no_bar}} no-bar, {{pt_not_run}} not-run).

A first pass by text search read the log very differently: {{v1_bar_first}} bar-first and {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit, with {{v1_no_bar}} no-bar and {{v1_unresolved}} unresolved. The cause was batch logging. A bar written earlier in a protocol, plan, scorer or specification often reached the log in the same commit as its result. Tracing each same-commit gate back to that earlier file moved {{sc_to_bf}} to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar (their bar cell declares none), and {{sc_stay}} stayed same-commit; the audit table gives the evidence line for every move. Rows that were already bar-first were not re-audited for a result hidden elsewhere. One open candidate remains, the reward-model progress test of 2026-11-01: same-commit by text search, although its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) is older than the log commit.

Point estimates give clean verdicts, and intervals do not always agree with them. Recomputing {{rc_rows}} early gates from stored rows with one statistic (episode bootstrap, {{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI) leaves {{rc_flips}} with the bar inside the interval. The no-learning colour judge is the plainest case. It is right on {{acc_est}} % of {{acc_n}} episodes against a {{acc_bar}} % pass mark, a {{acc_verdict}} on the point, yet its interval runs from {{acc_lo}} to {{acc_hi}} %. The oscillation rate among {{osc_n}} failed episodes is a {{osc_verdict}} ({{osc_est}} % against {{osc_bar}} %) with a lower bound of {{osc_lo}} %. A judge threshold's false-intervention rate is a {{tau_verdict}} at {{tau_est}} % of {{tau_n}} against a {{tau_bar}} % ceiling, with an upper bound of {{tau_hi}} %, and because that threshold was fitted to meet the rate, its interval only describes. The register keeps the pre-registered point verdicts and flags these three as fragile.

All {{l0}} write-ups rebuild from their published rows. Cold reruns are a stricter test. From a fresh clone with no credentials, {{l1_repro}} gates rerun and match, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. Another {{l1_env}} stop at the same place, the world-model environment, which is not published yet; publishing it would unblock most of them. The remaining {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint, the arm or a step the write-up leaves out. Citations and paper-side fields were checked against the papers' PDFs and corrected. Of the {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids the register names, those missing from our papers mirror all belong to scheduled tests, and the one tested paper without an arXiv id was not checked against a PDF.

## 3. Results

