{{TABLE1}}

Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} hold and {{p_fail}} fail (Table 1); {{p_not_proven}} are not proven, {{p_not_tested}} was not tested and {{p_withdrawn}} was withdrawn. By claim the picture is finer. Of {{claims}} claim rows ({{claims_tested}} tested), {{c_ours}} test our own baselines rather than a paper, such as "the commanded joints beat the world model", and {{c_ours_repro}} of those reproduce. The {{c_paper}} claims taken from papers split into {{c_paper_repro}} that reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} that fail. Over all rows: {{c_reproduces}} reproduce, {{c_fail}} fail, {{c_not_proven}} not proven, {{c_not_tested}} not tested, {{c_withdrawn}} withdrawn.

## 4. Three failure classes

We sort the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows by the condition each claim did not survive. Class A, the clock, is the easiest to see. The method may be sound, but on the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget the claim needs, as the adaptive chunking entry of §1 does ({{ex_a_ours}} per decision against {{ex_a_bar}}); for that entry nothing about the idea is refuted, the board is simply too slow for it.

Class B covers zero-shot judges, models used without training on this rig to judge success, failure or progress, that lose to a trivial signal on the same rows. A learned reward model detects failures at AUROC {{ex_b_ours}}, where episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. Class C is quicker to state: a learned latent used to plan, rank or track progress does worse than the commanded joints or a copy-last or action-only baseline (a world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, against a pass mark of {{ex_c_bar}} and {{ex_c_base}} for actions alone).

These examples illustrate the classes; they do not count them. A protocol committed before any labelling says the labels count only once a blind second reader, given the same {{fail_rows}} rows on the same form, agrees with the first reading at Cohen's κ ≥ {{kappa_bar}}. That reading has not happened yet.

## 5. Limitations

With a single operator, the author, running the rig and labelling outcomes at rest and not always blind to the arm, observer bias is the main confound. Two things limit it. Where commit order shows it, the pass mark predates the run, so a label could not have moved the bar. A blind two-reader protocol for the failure classes is also committed and ready, though it has not run, and until it does no verdict has a second reader. Ordering is provable for most gates, not all: for {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} ({{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar) the repository cannot show which came first, and {{sc_to_bf}} of the bar-first verdicts rest on matching a bar text in an earlier file. Under the bootstrap {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile. Cold reruns cover {{l1_repro}} gates so far, with the unpublished world-model environment as the main blocker. A failure here describes this rig, not the paper in its own setting, and because the papers were chosen for one stack the rate describes this register rather than the field.

## 6. Related work

Work on the same arm evaluates policies. A standardized real-world benchmark reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881], and a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool runs VLAs across 14 simulation benchmarks [@2603.13966]. A benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family [@2610.18869]. We test something else. Our targets are the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency), checked against thresholds fixed before the run, with our own pre-registration audited. The closest statement of our thesis [@2610.40026] scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records, seeds), and its authors state that it is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig instead.

## 7. Conclusion

A €{{rig_eur}} rig and ${{cost}} of compute were enough to re-test these claims and publish every row behind the verdicts, and {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} held. Money was never the constraint. Order was: keeping each pass mark ahead of its result, which our own history cannot prove for {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates. If you re-test a paper on a cheap rig, start with its cheapest baseline on the same rows, and commit the threshold before you look. Every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`).

